Ladybird uses an inline parent’s own font metrics and aligned baseline
for its client rectangles, even when it contains only atomic inline
children. Atomic children still contribute their inline-axis extent.
Their height, box edges, and own vertical alignment don’t determine
the parent’s font box. Sticky positioning consumes that corrected box.

Blink’s InlineBoxState::ComputeTextMetrics obtains the inline text
height and top from its font metrics. LayoutInline::CollectLineBoxRects
collects the resulting fragments. Gecko’s nsInlineFrame::ReflowFrames
uses nsLayoutUtils::SetBSizeFromFontMetrics for its own block size,
independently of its children’s heights. WebKit’s LineBoxBuilder::
setVerticalPropertiesForInlineLevelBox likewise sets an inline box’s
height from its own font ascent and descent. Ladybird follows that
separation between an inline font box and its atomic descendants.

The zero-font sticky reproduction matches Chrome: The parent has zero
height at the child’s baseline, and the child sits 30px above the
scrollport’s top after scrolling 150px. The WPT covers direct and nested
atomic children. Alignment controls cover a raised child, top- and
bottom-aligned parents, and top- and bottom-aligned atomic children.

Ladybird also uses the inline font box as the containing block for
an absolutely positioned overlay inside a linked image. With Ahem
at 16px, a 200x100 image gives its link and a 100%-height overlay
200x16 rectangles. The link reports its computed width and height,
including auto and percentages, while images and inline-blocks keep
reporting their used sizes. These cases match Chrome.

Blink’s ComputedStyleUtils::UsedBoxSize returns no used size for a
LayoutInline. Gecko’s nsComputedDOMStyle::DoGetWidth and DoGetHeight
exclude IsNonReplacedInline frames when reading used dimensions.
WebKit’s Style::PreferredSizeSharedAdaptor likewise excludes
non-replaced inlines before reading the sizing box. Ladybird applies
the same distinction when resolving width and height for CSSOM.
